1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:21,562
Shawna told me what happened in that room later. She told it sitting
beside me with her bare feet under my leg, stopping whenever she
wanted water and starting again wherever it hurt. I asked her the same
questions until she threatened to throw the glass at me. Some parts
she could give me word for word. Some parts she wouldn't give anybody.

2
00:00:21,812 --> 00:00:43,005
This is what she told me. Red took her back through the Saint after
we'd gone, with two men behind her and one hand around her arm. The
party hadn't completely emptied. A few people were still holding
drinks because nobody had personally told them the good time was dead.
She saw a woman by the elevators lift a phone.

3
00:00:43,255 --> 00:01:05,192
Get my good side, Shawna said. Red pulled her harder. The woman
lowered the phone. You always gotta do that? he asked. Look good? Make
a fucking scene. You dragged me out of one. He took her into a private
room she knew. White chairs. White carpet. A huge painting of a horse
coming out of the ocean. Red loved that painting.

4
00:01:05,442 --> 00:01:22,278
He'd once spent twenty minutes explaining the horse to her while she
tried to work out whether she could leave without him noticing.
Tonight the carpet had somebody else's blood on it. One of the men had
brought her purse from the office. Red took it from him and put it on
the table.

5
00:01:22,528 --> 00:01:42,788
Then he shut the door and told the men to wait outside. Shawna said
that was when she first got scared enough to feel cold. Not at the
gunfire. Not at his hand on her arm. At the little click of the door
and the fact that he wanted no one else to hear him talk. Sit, he
said. She stayed standing. Shawna.

6
00:01:43,038 --> 00:02:03,765
Give me a cigarette. He went to the table and took one from a silver
box. He lit it before he handed it to her, like he was bringing her
flowers. I could've killed that nigga, he said. She took the
cigarette. Nigga, you tried. Nigga ran. My nigga's coming back. Red
laughed. They always come back for the money.

7
00:02:04,015 --> 00:02:25,175
Then put me outside and see. He didn't answer that. He poured a drink,
missed the glass a little, and wiped the spill with his sleeve. Shawna
noticed because Red never cleaned anything. He would wait for somebody
to come across a whole room to pick up a thing beside his shoe. He was
angry enough to forget who he thought he was.

8
00:02:25,425 --> 00:02:47,107
You think that nigga loves you? She smoked. I asked you something. I
heard you. That nigga loves winning. You're what he stole. Soon as
you're just a woman in his house, watch what happens. She looked at
the painting instead of him. The horse had its mouth open. She'd
always thought it looked surprised to be swimming. He begged, you
know, Red said.

9
00:02:47,357 --> 00:03:08,934
She kept looking at the horse. Dante. That nigga begged us to look
after you. Us. Red stopped with the bottle in his hand. Shawna turned
toward him. You were standing there? I didn't say that. Then who told
you? He put the bottle down too carefully. Everybody knew what
happened. I didn't. He looked toward the door. She watched him do it.

10
00:03:09,184 --> 00:03:29,484
Shawna said she would've given anything to have the old letter and the
order in her hand right then, to lay them in his lap and make him
touch what he'd done. All she had was the watch. She turned it around
her wrist. What did he call me? she asked. What? When he begged. What
name did he use? Your fucking name. Which one?

11
00:03:29,734 --> 00:03:51,940
Shawna, I don't remember every— Dante called me Trouble. He stared at
her. Miss Trouble if he was happy. Mrs. Trouble when I was mad at him.
He'd come in and say, Mrs. Trouble, I fucked up, and I'd know I had to
sit down before that nigga finished talking. She laughed, and that
made Red angrier than anything she'd said. She couldn't help it.

12
00:03:52,190 --> 00:04:12,660
She was remembering Dante coming into their little apartment with a
whole bumper in his hands. He'd taken it off the car he'd borrowed for
their honeymoon. He'd brought the bumper upstairs because he thought
she might be less mad if he hadn't left any of it behind. Nigga
carried it like flowers, she told me later. She laughed then too.

13
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:31,710
I hated the dead nigga for making her laugh with something he'd done
years before I met her, and I wanted to hear it again anyway. In Red's
room, she wiped her eyes with the back of her wrist. You don't know
how he talked to me. I know what he was. Then talk about that. Talk
about your dead nigga.

14
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:52,453
You don't get to talk about mine. Red came close enough that she could
smell the drink. You keep making him a saint. No. I'm remembering my
husband. That nigga stole money, lied badly, and left his socks in the
bed. I loved him. You think telling me he was a bad man changes
something? She touched the watch again.

15
00:04:52,703 --> 00:05:16,292
Tell me something I don't fucking know. That nigga Dante said the same
shit, Red went on. A house. A different life. Music. That nigga was
gonna buy you a microphone and call himself a husband. Shawna turned
back. That nigga was my husband. I paid for the wedding. You paid for
the room. I paid for everything. Then you should've kept the receipts.

16
00:05:16,542 --> 00:05:37,036
Maybe the preacher would've married you. Red threw the glass. It hit
the wall beside the horse and broke. Shawna said she didn't move,
which she was proud of until she told me why. Her legs had gone stiff.
She couldn't move. He came toward her. You used to know how to talk to
me. I used to need you to stop talking.

17
00:05:37,286 --> 00:05:55,006
He caught her face in one hand. That's where the picture came from. He
called one of his men in and told him to take it while he held her
there. Shawna stared into the phone. He'll see you touching me, she
said. That's the point. I know. The man took the picture and left.

18
00:05:55,256 --> 00:06:17,581
Red's ring dragged against the edge of her jaw when he let go. She
looked at it. You wearing it now? His hand closed. Wearing what? All
that money, and you still want somebody to notice. He put his hand in
his pocket. A telephone rang outside the room, and Red's face changed
when the man at the door told him who was calling. Frost?

19
00:06:17,831 --> 00:06:39,698
Shawna asked. Red looked at her. She knew the name. Had heard it in
enough penthouse conversations to recognize which uniform Red meant
when he said the city would be reasonable. The man at the door
whispered something. Red took the phone so hard the man stepped
backward. What do you mean arrested? Shawna leaned back in the white
chair.

20
00:06:39,948 --> 00:07:03,018
Nigga, when she told me this part later, she made me listen twice.
Said the first time I'd smiled so much I hadn't heard the whole
sentence. Red kept listening. Knox said what? Shawna lifted her
cigarette. My captain? He covered the phone. Shut up. I'm just
wondering which nigga finally made your policeman nervous. He went
into the hallway to finish the call.

21
00:07:03,268 --> 00:07:25,849
Left the door open three inches. Left the man who'd taken the picture
inside with her. The man looked tired. Thirty-eight, name of Arlo, a
little gray beginning in his beard. She knew him too. He'd once
carried her groceries upstairs when the elevator broke and then
apologized because Red heard him asking whether she was all right.

22
00:07:26,099 --> 00:07:44,412
You don't have to stand in here, she told him. He looked at the door.
It's my job. Nigga, your job's turning into a funeral. He didn't
answer. She stood up with the cigarette in one hand. He moved toward
her. She lifted the other hand as if she only wanted him to stop
crowding her.

23
00:07:44,662 --> 00:08:07,978
Then she threw the heavy silver cigarette box into his face. Arlo went
back against the wall. She ran through the door, past Red with the
telephone still against his ear, and into the corridor before either
man understood that waiting quietly had ended. Red shouted her name.
She kept running. Her borrowed dress caught the corner of a table.

24
00:08:08,228 --> 00:08:30,131
She pulled free and left a strip of blue silk behind her. At the far
end, the service doors were open. Light came through from a stairwell.
She could hear men below, radios, a woman crying. She hit the door
with her shoulder and nearly fell over a waiter sitting on the
landing. He was holding his wrist. Blood marked his white cuff.

25
00:08:30,381 --> 00:08:52,302
Beside him stood a young-looking woman who told Shawna she was twenty-
four when Shawna asked if she could lift him. Both of them had been
trying to get past the men downstairs. Where's the outside door?
Shawna asked. Two floors, the woman said. But they won't let us— Get
him up. They lifted the waiter together. Shawna took his good side.

26
00:08:52,552 --> 00:09:14,998
The woman took the other. They started down the stairs, three people
moving awkwardly, the blue dress dragging through dust and somebody
else's dropped flowers. Behind them, Red came through the service
door. Shawna! She didn't look back. Nigga, if you want to help, get
his fucking feet. Red caught her at the next landing. The woman with
the waiter stopped.

27
00:09:15,248 --> 00:09:36,755
He pointed toward the stairs below. You two. Go. They stared. Go!
Shawna held on to the waiter until the woman had him steady. Then she
let them pass. She watched them reach the lower door, watched the
woman look up at her once, watched them disappear into the light. Red
grabbed her arm. You made me run. You should try it more.

28
00:09:37,005 --> 00:09:56,344
He pulled her toward him. Think you're him now? She looked at his
hand, then his face. Nigga, I know exactly who I am. That's what's
upsetting you. He tried to drag her back up the stairs. She planted
her feet and hit him with Dante's watch across the wrist holding her.
He swore and loosened his grip.

29
00:09:56,594 --> 00:10:15,911
She drove an elbow into his chest, got one step away, and nearly
reached the lower door. Arlo stood in it. One eye was swelling. Blood
ran from his nose. He looked at Shawna, looked at Red behind her, and
for one second she thought he might move. Then he raised his gun. She
stopped.

30
00:10:16,162 --> 00:10:35,230
He couldn't make himself look at her while he pointed it. I'm sorry,
he said. Then get better at it. Red pulled her back upstairs. Her
shoulder struck the rail. She bit the inside of her cheek to keep from
giving him a sound he could enjoy. When he put her back into the white
chair, the room looked different.

31
00:10:35,480 --> 00:10:55,892
The cigarette box lay on the floor. The little patch of carpet where
it had stood was clean and bright amid the mess. Red bent over her.
You get one more fucking idea— She looked up at him. I got several.
You're gonna need a longer night. He stepped away. Shawna told me her
hands were shaking after that.

32
00:10:56,142 --> 00:11:13,797
She put them under her thighs so he wouldn't see. She had failed to
get out. Two other people had made it. She held on to that while he
paced in front of her. When I asked what she'd been thinking, she put
her feet beneath my leg and looked at me like the answer ought to have
been obvious.

33
00:11:14,047 --> 00:11:36,072
What my nigga would've done, she said. Only in a better dress. At the
house, I didn't know any of this yet. Marcellus was still looking at
the same photograph, holding my phone so close to his face I thought
he might press his forehead through it. It's the stone, he said.
Nigga, tell me you sure. Yes. Nigga, we were sure at the party.

34
00:11:36,322 --> 00:11:57,491
He looked at me over the top of the phone. You were. I wanted to throw
him through the rest of the door. I also wanted him to be right more
than I'd wanted anything besides hearing her voice. Tell me, I said.
He set the decoy on the table. In that room it looked worth forty
million dollars. It looked worth killing for.

35
00:11:57,741 --> 00:12:19,773
Red could've shown it to a king, and the king would've started moving
money. Marcellus looked at it like an old sin. Nigga changed the
setting on the original. Put dark stones around it. Made it look
smaller by giving you other things to see. Nigga, you can read forty
million through my phone? I can tell you he didn't do a good job.

36
00:12:20,023 --> 00:12:42,675
His hands had steadied. For the first time that night, he was
somewhere he'd spent more years than prison. He turned the picture
toward the light. I saw the ring at the gala. Only from the side. I
thought I recognized the shoulder of the mounting. Here. He touched
the screen. The nigga kept that part. Because it's gold? Tank asked.

37
00:12:42,925 --> 00:13:06,210
Because that nigga's an idiot. Rosa let out a breath through her nose.
Finally, some good fucking news. Marcellus showed us where the blue
metal curved under the stone. Not a secret map. Not a number to
somebody's money. The old curve of a mounting he had shaped himself.
Red had reused one tiny part when he changed the necklace into a ring.

38
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:26,628
Marcellus knew the work the way I knew the sound of Shawna coming down
our stairs. He has her stone under his finger, he said. Nobody spoke
for a moment. Then I said, We'll get it. He looked at me. Will we, my
nigga? Say the whole promise. Yeah. If that nigga says her or the
diamond? Her.

39
00:13:26,878 --> 00:13:46,713
I didn't wait to see how that felt to him. If that nigga says burn the
letter? I burn it. If he says give him back the proof of what he did?
Then he gets it. You want me to make you a promise, I'll make you one.
I'm bringing her home. Everything else better find a way to come with
us. Marcellus nodded.

40
00:13:46,963 --> 00:14:10,662
His mouth tightened once. Dante said something very much like that. I
grabbed the edge of the table. Nigga, I'm not Dante. I know. Then stop
putting that nigga between me and every fucking thing I do. Rosa said
my name. I shook my head. No. I love her. Right now. Me. I'm the nigga
standing here bleeding. I'm the nigga going out there.

41
00:14:10,912 --> 00:14:28,761
You can remember him all you want, but when her phone rings, I want
her to know who's calling. My voice had gone loud enough to shake. I
hated all three of them hearing it. Marcellus took the papers we had
brought out of the gala and laid them beside Celeste's old letter.

42
00:14:29,011 --> 00:14:51,508
Rosa handed him the original order from Dante's watch, still inside
its envelope. He kept the different pages apart. One held a dead
woman's handwriting. The others held proof that Red had wanted another
man dead. They'd cost more nights than I could count, and I would have
set them all on fire if it got Shawna through that door.

43
00:14:51,758 --> 00:15:08,902
Then give her something to answer, he said. He slid my phone back to
me. I opened her name. The last message I'd sent before the gala was
still there. You look good as fuck. She had sent back a picture of one
eye, half her forehead, and her middle finger.

44
00:15:09,152 --> 00:15:31,305
She'd been trying to take a photograph of the whole dress and got mad
at the phone. I stared until the screen blurred. Rosa stood beside me.
She sent me six of those, she said quietly. Bitch got the bathroom
ceiling in one. I wiped my face with the heel of my hand. Nigga, I
whispered, though I wasn't talking to anybody now.

45
00:15:31,555 --> 00:15:52,384
I started typing. I'm coming. Wear whatever you want. Then I deleted
the second sentence. She couldn't choose what she was wearing. She
couldn't choose where she was sitting. Red would read it first, and I
wanted to take his head off for being between us even inside a fucking
phone. I sent only the first two words.

46
00:15:52,634 --> 00:16:09,481
At the Saint, Shawna said Red watched the message arrive. He held the
phone where she could see it and smiled. Nigga thinks he's in a love
song. Then don't stand in the chorus. He took the phone away. She
asked for her purse. He laughed at that.

47
00:16:09,730 --> 00:16:29,808
She asked for water instead, and he brought it because he wanted to
look like the kind of man who brought water. Tell me you were lying to
that nigga, he said. She drank. About what? All of it. That's a lot of
lying. You lied to Valentino. She set the glass down. Yeah. You lied
to me. Yeah.

48
00:16:30,058 --> 00:16:51,320
Then tell me you lied to that nigga. He waited so hard that Shawna
could see him believing her before she even opened her mouth. She said
that was the ugliest thing in the room. He'd hurt her, trapped her,
scared her, and still wanted her to make his heart feel better. She
almost gave him what he wanted. Not because it was true.

49
00:16:51,570 --> 00:17:12,048
Because she knew how to do it. She knew exactly how soft to make her
voice. She could've called him baby and watched his shoulders come
down. Instead she said, I told Aiden I wasn't scared. Red leaned
closer. There you go. I told him I didn't think about Dante when we
were together. He smiled. And?

50
00:17:12,298 --> 00:17:33,052
I told him I didn't like that stupid fucking cross. You see? You see
what I'm saying? She looked straight at him. I told him I could live
without him. His smile went away. She said his name then. Not mine.
Red's. She wanted him to know he was the one hearing it. That was a
lie. He knocked the water out of her hand.

51
00:17:33,302 --> 00:17:50,828
For a second she thought he was going to hit her again. He stood there
breathing, staring at the wet front of her dress. Then he picked up
his jacket. Get up. Where? You wanna see your nigga so bad, let's go
see him. He opened her purse and took out the little silver pistol.

52
00:17:51,078 --> 00:18:10,256
He looked at it, then at her, and put it in his jacket. Taking this
too, he said. He took her back into the hall. She left the cigarette
burning in his silver box. She told me that part twice. Of everything
he'd taken away from her that night, she wanted me to know he'd
forgotten to make her put it out.

53
00:18:10,506 --> 00:18:31,301
At ten thirty-seven, he sent me another message. On our way. Don't
disappoint her. I looked around the room at Tank, Rosa, Marcellus, and
the three million dollars that had already buried too many people.
Call everybody, I said. Tank picked up his phone. Everybody? Everybody
who ever called me brother.
